%S A401864 1,1,1,1,1,1,2,1,2,2,2,1,2,2,2,2,3,2,3,3,3,3,3,2,3,4,3,3,5,3,4,4,5,4,
%T A401864 4,3,5,5,4,4,5,4,6,5,5,6,6,5,6,7,5,5,8,5,6,7,6,6,7,6,6,8,7,6,9,6,8,9,
%U A401864 7,7,8,7,9,10,7,7,10,8,8,9,8,8,12,7,9,11,8,10,11,8,9,11,11,10,12,8,10,14,9,9
%N A401864 Number of coincidence site lattices of index 2n-1 in the cubic lattice Z^3, counted up to the rotations of the cube.
%C A401864 Equivalently, the number of coincidence rotations of Z^3 with coincidence index 2n-1, where two rotations R and R' are considered the same if R' = Q*R*Q' for some rotations Q and Q' of the cube.
%C A401864 Counting up to all 48 symmetries of the cube (reflections included) gives the same numbers, since every lattice is invariant under x -> -x.
%C A401864 The total number of these lattices is A031359(n), each of them coming from exactly 24 coincidence rotations; this sequence counts them up to the cube's rotations acting on both sides.
%C A401864 The terms shown were also obtained by direct enumeration of the lattices, with two independent programs.
%H A401864 Daniel Okwor, <a href="/A401864/b401864.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A401864 Michael Baake, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0605222">Solution of the coincidence problem in dimensions d <= 4</a>, arXiv:math/0605222 [math.MG], 2006; in R. V. Moody, ed., The Mathematics of Long-Range Aperiodic Order, Kluwer, Dordrecht, 1997, pp. 9-44.
%H A401864 Hans Grimmer, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1107/S0108767384000246">The generating function for coincidence site lattices in the cubic system</a>, Acta Cryst. A40 (1984), 108-112.
%H A401864 Daniel Okwor, <a href="https://github.com/OrygnsCode/cubic-coincidence-site-lattices">Programs and data for this sequence</a>, GitHub, 2026.
%H A401864 Peter Zeiner, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0605525">Symmetries of coincidence site lattices of cubic lattices</a>, arXiv:math/0605525 [math.MG], 2006; Z. Kristallogr. 220 (2005), 915-925.
%F A401864 a(1) = 1. For n >= 2, let S = 2n-1 have k distinct prime factors. Then a(n) = n1 + n2 + n3 + n4 + (A031359(n) - 4*n1 - 6*n2 - 8*n3 - 12*n4)/24, where n1 = 1 if S = 3 and 0 otherwise; n2 = 2^(k-1) if every prime factor of S is 1 mod 4, and 0 otherwise; n3 = 2^(j-1) if S > 3, 9 does not divide S, and the j >= 1 prime factors of S other than 3 are all 1 mod 6, and 0 otherwise; n4 = 2^(k-1) if S > 3 and every prime factor of S is 1 or 3 mod 8, and 0 otherwise (Zeiner, Theorem 2.2).
%e A401864 a(7) = 2: the A031359(7) = 14 coincidence site lattices of index 13 fall into two orbits under the rotations of the cube, one of size 6 and one of size 8.
%e A401864 a(17) = 3: the 48 coincidence site lattices of index 33 fall into three orbits, of sizes 12, 12 and 24.
%o A401864 (PARI) a(n) = {my(S = 2*n-1, f, p, k, psi, n1, n2 = 0, n3 = 0, n4 = 0, big);
%o A401864   if(S == 1, return(1));
%o A401864   f = factor(S); p = f[,1]; k = #p; psi = S*prod(i=1, k, 1+1/p[i]);
%o A401864   n1 = (S == 3);
%o A401864   if(prod(i=1, k, p[i]%4 == 1), n2 = 2^(k-1));
%o A401864   big = select(q -> q != 3, p~);
%o A401864   if(S > 3 && #big && prod(i=1, #big, big[i]%6 == 1) && valuation(S, 3) <= 1, n3 = 2^(#big-1));
%o A401864   if(S > 3 && prod(i=1, k, p[i]%8 == 1 || p[i]%8 == 3), n4 = 2^(k-1));
%o A401864   n1 + n2 + n3 + n4 + (psi - 4*n1 - 6*n2 - 8*n3 - 12*n4)/24;}
%Y A401864 Cf. A001615, A031359, A031360, A031361, A331139, A331140, A331141, A331142.
%K A401864 nonn,new
%O A401864 1,7
%A A401864 _Daniel Okwor_, Nov 07 2026
